WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:09.380
Keys should be generated through approved cryptographic libraries, platform facilities, or managed key services according to project policy.

2
00:00:09.480 --> 00:00:13.720
Generation is too important to improvise inside ordinary application logic.

3
00:00:14.000 --> 00:00:23.600
Do not generate keys with timestamps, counters, predictable seeds, general-purpose random functions, or homemade entropy collection.

4
00:00:23.700 --> 00:00:30.420
Those patterns can produce values that look random enough in a log or test but do not provide the assurance the design needs.

5
00:00:30.700 --> 00:00:36.060
Key length and algorithm compatibility should follow current project and platform policy.

6
00:00:36.160 --> 00:00:41.680
Application code should not guess sizes or silently downgrade choices for convenience.

7
00:00:41.780 --> 00:00:46.900
If compatibility constraints exist, document them and review them deliberately.

8
00:00:47.180 --> 00:00:49.420
Related values have different roles.

9
00:00:49.520 --> 00:00:55.440
Salts, IVs, nonces, initialization values, and random challenges are not interchangeable.

10
00:00:55.540 --> 00:00:59.060
Some are public but must be unique.

11
00:00:59.160 --> 00:01:01.080
Some need unpredictability.

12
00:01:01.180 --> 00:01:04.620
Some belong to a specific algorithm or library mode.

13
00:01:04.900 --> 00:01:08.580
Nonce or IV policy should be tied to the approved API.

14
00:01:08.680 --> 00:01:16.760
The code should make it clear whether the library generates the value, whether the application supplies it, and how uniqueness or persistence is maintained.

15
00:01:17.040 --> 00:01:22.320
Generation should be automated, auditable, and repeatable as a controlled process.

16
00:01:22.420 --> 00:01:29.540
A production deployment should not depend on a developer hand-generating a key on a workstation and pasting it into configuration.

17
00:01:29.820 --> 00:01:39.420
The review question is simple: where did this key come from, was the source approved, and can the team prove the process will remain safe during rotation, scaling, and recovery?

18
00:01:39.700 --> 00:01:43.060
Automation also reduces drift between environments.

19
00:01:43.160 --> 00:01:50.600
If each team invents a different generation process, policies become hard to verify and emergency replacement becomes slower.

20
00:01:50.880 --> 00:01:59.120
Controlled generation should produce metadata as well as key material, including owner, purpose, environment, and creation context.
